# Independent review of the Vault bin-view correction

Candidate `work/keepass-recycle-bin-generation-fix-next` receipt `316a97f3565823f007bd9a8e66167f453d249747bc06ffa14132fe7cfd07d4e5`, strict208 manifest `17e9757a0bf58173c433465b12bf2a10a62570b1b5e85bd38bad29c96b88f775` was authenticated before and after this review. All current candidate artifacts and prior independent61 artifacts remain unchanged. The candidate client is exactly the old client with two `viewCurrent()` conditions replaced by `binCurrent()` after the active-entry-list await. Its other five runtime modules are byte-identical to the original546 stage.

The original five independent test bodies are byte-identical (`c095a6eadde6805eac2f7b50806f695aa95aa2794f8d86d5024aa7a80653354a`); only the helper's stage path is retargeted. All five now pass, including Close during Restore/Purge and newer-bin-preview retention. Two fresh unchecked-confirmation controls pass without persisted mutation, leaving a read-only preview usable. Total7/7,zero skips. These races execute actual candidate client/server via owned DOM and held transport, not delayed browser HTTP.

Actual Chrome on one owned ephemeral shared host also passed the ordinary sequence: open synthetic entry, confirm movement into the bin, inspect masked read-only preview, confirm previous-group restore, retain its one history row, lock/unlock and reopen with group/body/history intact. Screenshots and accessibility states are retained. The owned tab was closed; the host exited normally after a `close` message and removed its verified synthetic temporary directory. User4311/root hosts were untouched. This campaign executes no original assembly; the prior independent original4-process and native6-group checks remain separately bound to unchanged backend bytes, not recounted as fresh executions.

The author's failed same-generation-preview diagnostic had an invalid premise: the whole-graph token was already stale, so no successful newer preview existed to erase. Its failed source/raw remain preserved in the author stage and are explicitly distinguished from the three real original failures. No bin-interaction counter was added. This review confirms the bounded two-guard correction, not universal lifecycle or product parity.

Own preparation failure is retained under `failures/manifest-schema-v1`: the first harness expected `files` rather than the author's `records` field, so no candidate case ran; a chained test invocation then reported a missing test file. Corrected preparation authenticated208 and kept61 old artifacts unchanged before the7 actual cases. The failure is attributed only to review tooling.

Finite acceptance: the three confirmed original UI failures are resolved by this candidate under the unchanged cases. Native/release semantics, KDBX4.0 vs conditional4.1 boundary, entry-only subset, resource bounds and prior limitations remain as documented in the original frozen review. No full compatibility, security, performance or superiority claim is made.
